{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias at short-to-mid horizons (≈D+1 to D+20): model underpredicts during rising regimes; apply an upward level adjustment of roughly +1 to +3 €/t when the last 5–10 days show positive momentum.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts respond too slowly to sharp up-moves (miss the jump and subsequent elevated plateau). If a 1–3 day rally >~2 €/t occurs, shift the next ~10–15 forecast days upward and steepen the near-term slope (don’t fade immediately).",
    "Systematic underestimation of peaks: during strong rallies the model caps too low (misses 72–73 area in examples). Add a convexity/peak premium in the first half of the horizon when volatility is elevated (e.g., widen upside by +1–2 €/t).",
    "Over-optimism at long horizons (≈D+25 to D+30): model often projects continued strength while realized prices mean-revert lower. Apply a downward taper to the tail (reduce last 5–7 days by ~2–6 €/t, stronger when the model tail is above recent 10-day mean).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when recent prices have spiked up, the model extrapolates too high into the far tail; enforce mean reversion toward the 10–20 day average beyond ~3 weeks.",
    "Regime-switch miss (late sell-off): model fails to anticipate sharp drawdowns following elevated levels; if spot is above the 20-day mean by >~2 €/t and momentum turns negative, bias the entire path downward and accelerate the decline beyond ~D+15."
  ]
}
